course: dictionary
lesson: 8932
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The words around them show you which meaning the writer wants. Signer has two meanings: a person who signs their name on something, and a person who uses sign language. «BSL, Auslan and New Zealand Sign Language belong to one family, so their signers understand each other quite well.» Here, signers are people who use sign language, because the sentence is about languages.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. Could a signer be someone who signs their name here? No, the sentence is about languages that the hands and faces use. The clue is the word family, and the words around it, which show the topic.
explain: «Faces matter as much as hands: raised eyebrows can turn a sentence into a question, so a signer's expressions are grammar, not rudeness.» Hands and faces are the clues, so this signer is a person who uses sign language. Expressions are part of the language, so they are not bad manners.
fact: A signer is a person who signs their name on something, like a letter, card or important document.
fact: A signer is also a person who uses sign language, especially one who interprets speech into sign language for deaf people.
fact: To interpret speech means to change spoken words into sign language so that deaf people can follow them.
fact: BSL is British Sign Language, ASL is American Sign Language, and Auslan is Australian Sign Language.
fact: Sign languages have their own grammar, and the face is part of it.
exercise: «The signer of the card wrote her name at the bottom.» What is a signer in this sentence? | A card maker | A person who uses sign language | A pen for writing | *A person who signs their name on something
exercise: Mia watches the TV news, and a signer stands beside the speaker. What is the signer doing? | Writing the news on the screen | Taking photos of the speaker | *Translating speech into sign language for deaf viewers | Reading the news out loud to the room
exercise: «Faces matter as much as hands.» What does this tell you about a signer's face? | Faces should stay still and blank | *Raised eyebrows and expressions are part of the grammar | Faces are only for looking pretty | Faces are not used in sign language
test: «BSL, Auslan and New Zealand Sign Language belong to one family, so their signers understand each other quite well.» What do these signers have in common? | *They use sign language | They sign their names on documents | They all work on the same TV channel | They cannot understand each other
test: Which sentence uses signer to mean a person who signs their name? | BSL and Auslan belong to one family | A signer stood beside the speaker on TV | The signers used their hands and faces | *The signer of the form must sign at the bottom
exam: Ben says signer means the same thing in «a signer stands next to the speaker» and «the signer of the letter wrote her name». Is he right? | No, a signer is always a famous person | Yes, both mean a person who writes | Yes, both mean a person who waves | *No, the first is a person who uses sign language and the second is a person who signs their name
